```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: red
REAL_USE_CASE: Multimodal LLM serving operators (e.g., Qwen2-VL on vLLM-V1/SGLang) running chunked prefill with encoder-cache enabled, processing requests with small total image-token counts (T<=512) and varying image counts (k=1 vs k=8), who track worst-iteration ITL stall for individual requests rather than stream-p99 over a decode window.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only. The L0 simulation confirms a mechanism (image-count-driven ITL inflation) but the material worst-iter stall (1.25-5.9x at realistic r~64-1024, T~256-1280) is invisible to the production-relevant stream-p99 SLO (1.0x at same T, B=512). The prereg's PRIMARY metric (stream p99) shows materiality only at large T>>B where worst-iter washes out. vLLM PR#8425 already disabled multimodal+chunked-prefill by default, contradicting the packet's "default post-encoder-cache" deployment assumption, so the regime is not active in production.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [vLLM PR#8425 explicitly disabled multimodal+chunked-prefill ("doesn't work well"); encoder-cache PRs fixed recompute but did not re-enable the feature by default; scheduler requires non-trivial modification to account for per-image fixed ViT overhead (k*per_image_fixed) or to chunk ViT atomically; real r and B unmeasured—L0 magnitude is f(anchored-but-UNMEASURED r, chunk size B); worst-iteration metric is not a standard production SLO (Sarathi-Serve defines ITL p99 over window); stream-p99 (the SLO) shows no effect at realistic Qwen2-VL T, making the fix low priority for maintainers]
RATIONALE: Packet admits "vLLM PR#8425 ALREADY says 'multimodal+chunked-prefill doesn't work well'" and the claim's defense is a "DIFFERENT axis" (image-COUNT-driven latency) surviving the encoder-cache recompute fix. L0 shows the mechanism is real (anti-circular r=0 → 1.000) but REGIME-BOUNDED: "worst-iteration ITL (single stall) material at SMALL T (T <= B)" while "stream p99 over 400-iter window material at LARGE T (many chunks)" — the prereg's PRIMARY prediction did NOT hold. For realistic Qwen2-VL (r~64-1024, T~256-1280), worst-iter is 1.25-5.9x but stream-p99 is 1.0x (packet Table: r=64-1024, T=256-1024 → 1.000). The operational SLO is stream-p99, not worst-iter; the "devastating" stall "hides below stream-p99 in a busy decode pool." Deployment regime is questionable: PR#8425 disabled the feature, and the packet's "default post-encoder-cache" is unverified. L0 is CPU-only simulation; "real magnitude needs the L1 microbench of real r + B" (packet). Negative result is actionable: for p99 SLOs at realistic T, token budget IS sufficient (null region at T>>B for worst-iter, and at T<=B for stream-p99), confirming encoder-cache restored stall-free guarantee for the metric that matters. (v001)
```
